1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,850
Labyrinths have existed for well over 4,000 years.

2
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:13,496
Labyrinths and labyrinthine symbols have been found in regions as diverse as modern-day Turkey,

3
00:00:13,496 --> 00:00:15,545
Ireland, Greece,

4
00:00:15,545 --> 00:00:16,910
and India.

5
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:21,766
There are various designs of labyrinth but what they all have in common is

6
00:00:21,766 --> 00:00:26,040
a winding spiral path which leads to a central area.

7
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:30,843
There is one starting point at the entrance and the goal is to reach

8
00:00:30,843 --> 00:00:32,250
the central area.

9
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:36,988
Finding your way through a labyrinth involves many twists and turns,

10
00:00:36,988 --> 00:00:42,530
but it’s not possible to get lost as there is only one single

11
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:42,580
path.

12
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:47,413
In modern times, the word labyrinth has taken on a different meaning

13
00:00:47,413 --> 00:00:50,990
and is often used as a synonym for a maze.

14
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:55,950
A maze is quite different as it is a kind of puzzle with an

15
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:58,280
intricate network of paths.

16
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:03,096
Mazes became fashionable in the 15th and 16th centuries in Europe,

17
00:01:03,096 --> 00:01:07,430
and can still be found in the gardens of great houses and palaces.

18
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:13,024
The paths are usually surrounded by thick, high hedges so that it’s not possible

19
00:01:13,024 --> 00:01:14,010
to see over them.

20
00:01:14,010 --> 00:01:20,891
Entering a maze usually involves getting lost a few times before using logic to work out the pattern

21
00:01:20,891 --> 00:01:24,060
and find your way to the centre and then out again.

22
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:28,706
There are lots of dead ends and paths which lead you back to where

23
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:29,350
you started.

24
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:34,836
The word ‘maze’ is believed to come from a Scandinavian word for a state

25
00:01:34,836 --> 00:01:35,640
of confusion.

26
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,830
This is where the word ‘amazing’ comes from.

27
00:01:39,830 --> 00:01:44,980
Labyrinths, on the other hand, have a very different function.

28
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:51,563
Although people now often refer to things they find complicated as labyrinths, this is

29
00:01:51,563 --> 00:01:53,570
not how they were seen in the past.

30
00:01:53,570 --> 00:01:59,394
The winding spiral of the labyrinth has been used for centuries as a metaphor

31
00:01:59,394 --> 00:02:00,880
for life’s journey.

32
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:06,265
It served as a spiritual reminder that there is purpose and meaning to our lives

33
00:02:06,265 --> 00:02:09,580
and helped to give people a sense of direction.

34
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:15,283
Labyrinths are thought to encourage a feeling of calm and have been used as a meditation

35
00:02:15,283 --> 00:02:19,360
and prayer tool in many cultures over many centuries.

36
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:25,535
The earliest examples of the labyrinth spiral pattern have been found carved into stone,

37
00:02:25,535 --> 00:02:31,530
from Sardinia to Scandinavia, from Arizona to India to Africa.

38
00:02:31,530 --> 00:02:36,610
In Europe, these spiral carvings date from the late Bronze Age.

39
00:02:36,610 --> 00:02:43,924
The Native American Pima tribe wove baskets with a circular labyrinth design that depicted

40
00:02:43,924 --> 00:02:45,210
their own cosmology.

41
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:52,029
In Ancient Greece, the labyrinth spiral was used on coins around four thousand years

42
00:02:52,029 --> 00:02:52,170
ago.

43
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:57,831
Labyrinths made of mosaics were commonly found in bathhouses, villas

44
00:02:57,831 --> 00:03:00,320
and tombs throughout the Roman Empire.

45
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:06,620
In Northern Europe, there were actual physical labyrinths designed for walking on.

46
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:12,010
These were cut into the turf or grass, usually in a circular pattern.

47
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:15,887
The origin of these walking labyrinths remains unclear,

48
00:03:15,887 --> 00:03:20,789
but they were probably used for fertility rites which may date back thousands of

49
00:03:20,789 --> 00:03:20,970
years.

50
00:03:20,970 --> 00:03:28,545
Eleven examples of turf labyrinths survive today, including the large stone at Saffron Walden,

51
00:03:28,545 --> 00:03:28,985
England,

52
00:03:28,985 --> 00:03:36,600
which used to have a large tree in the middle of it.

53
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:41,190
More recently labyrinths have experienced something of a revival.

54
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:45,282
Some believe that walking a labyrinth promotes healing and mindfulness,

55
00:03:45,282 --> 00:03:48,873
and there are those who believe in its emotional and physical benefits,

56
00:03:48,873 --> 00:03:54,490
which include slower breathing and a restored sense of balance and perspective.

57
00:03:54,490 --> 00:04:01,651
This idea has become so popular that labyrinths have been laid into the floors of spas, wellness centres

58
00:04:01,651 --> 00:04:04,520
and even prisons in recent years.

59
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:12,964
A pamphlet at Colorado Children’s Hospital informs patients that ‘walking a labyrinth can often

60
00:04:12,964 --> 00:04:15,170
calm people in the midst of a crisis’.

61
00:04:15,170 --> 00:04:19,300
And apparently, it’s not only patients who benefit.

62
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:24,244
Many visitors find walking a labyrinth less stressful than sitting in a corridor

63
00:04:24,244 --> 00:04:25,490
or waiting room.

64
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:29,970
Some doctors even walk the labyrinth during their breaks.

65
00:04:29,970 --> 00:04:35,576
In some hospitals, patients who can’t walk can have a paper ‘finger labyrinth’ brought

66
00:04:35,576 --> 00:04:36,500
to their bed.

67
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,253
The science behind the theory is a little sketchy,

68
00:04:39,253 --> 00:04:44,416
but there are dozens of small-scale studies which support claims about the benefits of

69
00:04:44,416 --> 00:04:45,240
labyrinths.

70
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:51,840
For example, one study found that walking a labyrinth provided ‘short-term calming, relaxation,

71
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:55,150
and relief from anxiety’ for Alzheimer’s patients.

72
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:59,931
So, what is it about labyrinths that makes their appeal so universal?

73
00:04:59,931 --> 00:05:02,000
Well …
